            takeaway: "Pressure may be manageable if the new loan remains short and affordable."
          },
          {
            label: "Large rolled balance",
            takeaway: "Pressure rises when old debt is added to a depreciating vehicle."
          },
          {
            label: "Cash down available",
            takeaway: "Reducing negative equity can lower long-term risk."
          }
        ]}
      commonMistake="Focusing on the new monthly payment while ignoring old debt rolled into the loan."
      nextStep="Compare trade now, pay down first, and keep the car longer."
    />



    <section>
      <h2>Why Negative Equity Is So Easy to Miss</h2>

      <p>
        A dealer can make an underwater trade feel simple because the old payoff gets
        handled inside the new deal. The danger is that the borrower may leave with a
        higher loan balance than the new vehicle is worth from the start.
      </p>

      <p>
        That matters because a high loan-to-value position can limit refinancing options,
        make the car harder to sell, increase gap-insurance need, and keep the next trade-in
        stuck in the same cycle.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What the Calculator Checks</h2>

      <div class="info-grid">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Current payoff gap</h3>
          <p>The difference between payoff and trade value shows the raw underwater amount.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Cash used to fix it</h3>
          <p>Cash, rebates, reimbursement, or outside help can reduce the negative equity before it is financed.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>New loan amount</h3>
          <p>The new loan includes vehicle price, fees, add-ons, taxes, and any remaining rolled balance.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Loan-to-value</h3>
          <p>A loan above the new vehicle price can signal old debt, high fees, or too little down payment.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Payment pressure</h3>
          <p>The estimated payment is tested against take-home income, essentials, debt, and insurance change.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Savings runway</h3>
          <p>Cash used to escape negative equity is safer when emergency savings remain strong afterward.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How to Read the Result</h2>

      <h3>If rolled negative equity is zero</h3>
      <p>
        The trade is not carrying old vehicle debt into the new loan. The decision still
        needs a normal car affordability check, but the negative-equity problem is not
        the main issue.
      </p>

      <h3>If rolled negative equity is small</h3>
      <p>
        A small rolled balance can still be risky if the term is long, APR is high, or
        savings are thin. The payment may be manageable while the loan position remains weak.
      </p>

      <h3>If rolled negative equity is large</h3>
      <p>
        Large negative equity can turn a new vehicle into a debt reset. In that case,
        keeping the current car longer, selling privately, saving cash, or choosing a much
        cheaper replacement may be safer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="FinancialRedFlags">
      <h2>Negative Equity Car Loan Red Flags</h2>

      <ul class="red-flag-list">
        <li>The new loan amount is higher than the new vehicle price before taxes and fees.</li>
        <li>The old car is still drivable but the trade is being rushed to escape boredom or payment fatigue.</li>
        <li>The loan term is 72, 84, or 96 months because the rolled balance makes the payment too high.</li>
        <li>The APR is high and negative equity is being financed instead of paid down.</li>
        <li>Emergency savings are thin after cash is used for the new deal.</li>
        <li>The replacement payment only works before the insurance increase is included.</li>
        <li>Add-ons, warranties, fees, and old debt are all being packed into one larger loan.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="CalculatorAssumptions">
      <h2>Negative Equity Calculator Assumptions</h2>

      <p>
        This calculator estimates negative equity by subtracting trade-in or sale value
        from the current payoff, then subtracting cash, rebate, reimbursement, or outside
        help used specifically to cover the gap. Any remaining gap is treated as rolled
        negative equity.
      </p>

      <p>
        The estimated payment uses the loan amount, APR, and term entered. It is not a
        lender quote and does not include every possible tax, title, registration, fee,
        add-on, insurance premium, or dealer product unless those amounts are entered.
      </p>
    </section>


    <section id="NegativeEquityMeaning">
      <h2>What Negative Equity Means on a Car Loan</h2>

      <p>
        Negative equity means the amount needed to pay off the current auto loan is
        higher than the car's realistic trade-in or sale value. If the lender payoff is
        $24,000 and the dealer offers $18,000, the vehicle is $6,000 underwater. That
        $6,000 does not disappear when the car is traded. It has to be covered with cash,
        absorbed by a rebate or other credit, or added to the next loan.
      </p>

      <div class="formula-box">
        <p>
          <strong>Basic negative-equity formula:</strong><br />
          Current loan payoff − vehicle value = negative equity
        </p>
        <p>
          Example: $24,000 payoff − $18,000 trade value = <strong>$6,000 negative equity</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        The number that matters most is not the dealer's monthly-payment quote. It is the
        amount of old debt that remains after the trade value and any cash specifically
        used to close the gap. That remaining balance becomes part of the financing on
        the next vehicle.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="TradeInRollover">
      <h2>What Happens When Negative Equity Is Rolled Into a New Car Loan?</h2>

      <p>
        Rolling negative equity into a new loan means financing part of the old car after
        the old car is gone. Suppose a replacement vehicle costs $35,000 and $5,000 of
        negative equity is added to the deal. Before taxes, fees, warranties, or other
        add-ons, the financing can already be based on $40,000 rather than $35,000.
      </p>

      <p>
        This can create three problems at once. The loan balance starts higher, the
        payment usually rises, and the borrower can begin the new loan owing more than
        the replacement car is worth. A longer term may reduce the monthly payment, but
        it can also keep the loan underwater for longer because principal falls slowly
        while the vehicle continues to depreciate.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is why the calculator shows both the rolled balance and loan-to-value. A
        payment can fit the monthly budget while the loan itself remains difficult to
        refinance, sell, or trade later.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="NegativeEquityOptions">
      <h2>Four Ways to Deal With an Underwater Car</h2>

      <div class="decision-grid">
        <div class="decision-card">
          <h3>Keep the current car longer</h3>
          <p>
            Continuing normal payments can reduce the payoff balance while giving the
            vehicle value time to catch up. This is often the cleanest option when the
            car is reliable and the replacement is optional.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="decision-card">
          <h3>Pay down the gap before trading</h3>
          <p>
            Extra principal payments can shrink the amount that would otherwise roll
            forward. Compare this approach with the
            <a href="/car-loan-early-payoff-calculator/">car loan early payoff calculator</a>
            before using a large lump sum.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="decision-card">
          <h3>Use cash to cover negative equity</h3>
          <p>
            Cash can keep old debt out of the next loan, but the trade is weaker if doing
            so empties emergency savings. Test the remaining cushion before treating a
            zero-rollover deal as automatically safe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="decision-card">
          <h3>Trade now and roll part of it</h3>
          <p>
            Sometimes replacement cannot wait because of safety, reliability, work, or
            family transportation needs. In that case, keeping the replacement price,
            APR, term, and add-ons under control becomes especially important.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="DownPaymentVsNegativeEquity">
      <h2>Does a Down Payment Cancel Out Negative Equity?</h2>

      <p>
        A down payment can improve the new loan, but only after the old-car gap is accounted
        for. If a trade has $6,000 of negative equity and the buyer brings $4,000, there can
        still be $2,000 of old debt left to finance before the normal down payment on the
        replacement vehicle is considered.
      </p>

      <p>
        This distinction is easy to miss because dealership paperwork can combine trade
        allowance, payoff, rebates, cash down, fees, and the replacement price into one
        transaction. The calculator separates the negative-equity coverage from the new-car
        down payment so it is easier to see whether cash is actually repairing the old loan
        or reducing the new vehicle balance.
      </p>

      <p>
        Before using a large amount of savings, compare the result with the
        <a href="/how-much-should-i-put-down-on-a-car/">car down payment calculator</a> and
        the <a href="/cash-vs-finance-car-calculator/">cash vs finance car calculator</a>.
        A lower loan balance helps, but not if the household loses the cash needed for the
        next repair, deductible, or income interruption.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="NegativeEquityExamples">
      <h2>Negative Equity Examples: When the Same Gap Creates Different Risk</h2>

      <div class="scenario-grid">
        <div class="scenario-card">
          <h3>$3,000 underwater with strong savings</h3>
          <p>
            A modest gap may be manageable when the buyer can cover it without draining
            savings and the new loan remains short, reasonably priced, and affordable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="scenario-card">
          <h3>$8,000 underwater on a 72-month replacement loan</h3>
          <p>
            The rolled balance can keep the new car underwater for years. The payment may
            look acceptable while the borrower has little flexibility to sell or refinance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="scenario-card">
          <h3>$5,000 underwater but the current car still works</h3>
          <p>
            Waiting can be valuable because additional payments reduce the payoff without
            creating a new depreciation cycle. Compare keeping the vehicle with the
            <a href="/should-i-trade-in-my-car-or-keep-it/">trade-in or keep-it guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="scenario-card">
          <h3>$5,000 underwater and replacement is urgent</h3>
          <p>
            If the current car is unsafe or unreliable, waiting may not be practical. The
            safer version of the replacement is usually the one with the lowest total
            ownership pressure, not simply the smallest quoted payment.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="DealerMath">
      <h2>Dealer Trade-In Math to Check Before Signing</h2>

      <p>
        A trade-in deal can be difficult to read because several numbers move at the same
        time. Ask for the current lender payoff, the actual trade allowance, the replacement
        vehicle selling price, taxes and fees, add-ons, cash down, APR, term, and final
        amount financed as separate figures.
      </p>

      <p>
        Then compare the payoff with the trade value yourself. A higher trade allowance is
        not automatically a better deal if the replacement vehicle price or fees also rise.
        Likewise, a lower payment is not automatically progress if the term gets longer or
        old debt is hidden inside a larger amount financed.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <a href="/total-cost-of-car-ownership-calculator/">total cost of car ownership calculator</a>
        is useful after the loan structure is clear because it adds insurance, fuel,
        maintenance, repairs, fees, and depreciation to the payment.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="LoanToValue">
      <h2>Why Loan-to-Value Matters When You Are Already Underwater</h2>

      <p>
        Loan-to-value, or LTV, compares the amount financed with the value or price of the
        replacement vehicle. A high LTV can make the next loan harder to escape because
        there is less equity available if the car must be sold, totaled, refinanced, or
        traded again.
      </p>

      <p>
        Rolled negative equity, taxes, fees, warranties, service contracts, and a small down
        payment can all push the amount financed above the vehicle price. Longer terms can
        compound the problem by slowing principal reduction. That is why an
        <a href="/should-i-finance-a-car-for-84-months/">84-month car loan</a> deserves extra
        scrutiny when negative equity is already part of the deal.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="GapInsurance">
      <h2>Negative Equity and GAP Coverage Are Related, but They Are Not the Same Thing</h2>

      <p>
        GAP coverage can help with a covered total loss when an auto insurance settlement is
        lower than the loan payoff, subject to the policy's terms, limits, exclusions, and
        eligibility rules. It does not make an expensive loan affordable, erase old debt in
        a normal trade, or guarantee that every financed amount will be covered.
      </p>

      <p>
        Treat GAP as an insurance question, not a fix for the underlying loan structure.
        Verify the actual policy or waiver terms before relying on it, especially when the
        loan includes rolled negative equity, add-ons, or a very high loan-to-value ratio.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="WhenTradingNowMakesSense">
      <h2>When Trading an Underwater Car Can Still Be Reasonable</h2>

      <p>
        Negative equity is a warning sign, not an automatic command to keep the current car
        forever. A replacement can be reasonable when the current vehicle is unsafe,
        unreliable, expensive to keep repairing, unsuitable for necessary transportation,
        or likely to create a larger financial problem if it fails.
      </p>

      <p>
        In those cases, compare the cost of staying with the cost of leaving. Use the
        <a href="/car-repair-vs-replace-calculator/">repair vs replace calculator</a> when
        repairs are driving the decision, and keep the replacement as inexpensive and
        structurally clean as practical. The goal is to solve the transportation problem
        without turning one underwater loan into a larger second one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="NegativeEquityActionPlan">
      <h2>A Simple Negative Equity Action Plan</h2>

      <p>
        Start by getting the lender payoff and a realistic vehicle value. Calculate the
        gap. Then decide how much of that gap can be covered without weakening emergency
        savings. Only after that should the replacement vehicle, APR, term, insurance
        change, and total ownership cost enter the decision.
      </p>

      <p>
        If the deal only works by rolling a large balance, stretching to a long term, or
        using most available cash, the problem is probably not the calculator. The deal
        itself needs to change. A cheaper replacement, more time, a better trade value,
        additional principal payments, or a cleaner loan structure can improve the result
        more than negotiating around the monthly payment.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="MethodologySection">
      <h2>How ShouldISpend Calculates Negative Equity Pressure</h2>

      <p>
        ShouldISpend calculates raw negative equity, covered negative equity, rolled
        negative equity, new loan amount, estimated payment, loan-to-value, payment share
        of income, emergency savings after cash used, and monthly room after essentials,
        other debt, insurance change, and the new payment.
      </p>

      <p>
        The pressure score can reach zero when there is no rolled negative equity or the
        negative equity is fully covered while emergency savings remain objectively strong.
        It can reach 100 when large negative equity is rolled into a long or high-APR loan
        with thin savings, high monthly pressure, and little room to recover.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="RelatedCalculators">
      <h2>Related Car Calculators</h2>

      <div class="link-grid">
        <a class="link-card" href="/car-calculators/">
          <strong>Car Payment Calculators</strong>
          <span>See the full car calculator cluster for payments, repairs, insurance, loan length, and ownership pressure.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/car-repair-vs-replace-calculator/">
          <strong>Car Repair vs Replace Calculator</strong>
          <span>Decide whether to repair, replace, or get another quote before rolling old debt into a new vehicle.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/total-cost-of-car-ownership-calculator/">
          <strong>Total Cost of Car Ownership Calculator</strong>
          <span>See the real monthly cost after payment, insurance, fuel, maintenance, repairs, fees, and depreciation.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-trade-in-my-car-or-keep-it/">
          <strong>Should I Trade In My Car or Keep It?</strong>
          <span>Compare keeping a vehicle with trading it in before making a larger ownership decision.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-finance-a-car-for-84-months/">
          <strong>84-Month Car Loan Calculator</strong>
          <span>Test whether a long loan is lowering the payment while raising interest and underwater risk.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/how-much-car-can-i-afford/">
          <strong>How Much Car Can I Afford?</strong>
          <span>Build a vehicle budget from income, savings, debt, insurance, loan structure, and monthly pressure.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/cash-vs-finance-car-calculator/">
          <strong>Cash vs Finance Car Calculator</strong>
          <span>Compare using cash with financing while protecting emergency savings and monthly flexibility.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/car-loan-early-payoff-calculator/">
          <strong>Car Loan Early Payoff Calculator</strong>
          <span>See whether paying down the current loan first can reduce interest and shrink the underwater gap.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/how-much-should-i-put-down-on-a-car/">
          <strong>Car Down Payment Calculator</strong>
          <span>Test how much cash to put down without weakening the emergency fund.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Journey Wave 2A negative equity traffic expansion -->
    <FAQSection title="Negative Equity Car Loan FAQ" faqs={negativeEquityFaqs} />
  </main>

  <script is:inline>
    document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function () {
      const button = document.getElementById("calculateNegativeEquity");

      if (!button) return;

      const moneyLabelStyle =
        "color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.82rem; font-weight: 900; letter-spacing: 0.04em; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 6px;";

      const moneyValueStyle =
        "color: var(--primary); font-size: clamp(2.45rem, 6vw, 3.75rem); font-weight: 950; line-height: 1.04; letter-spacing: -0.055em; margin: 4px 0 16px; display: block;";

      const moneySubtextStyle =
        "color: var(--text); font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 850; line-height: 1.55; margin-bottom: 16px;";

      function cleanNumber(id) {
        const element = document.getElementById(id);
        if (!element) return 0;
        const value = Number(element.value);
        return Number.isFinite(value) && value > 0 ? value : 0;
      }

      function money(value) {
        const sign = value < 0 ? "-" : "";
        return sign + "$" + Math.round(Math.abs(value)).toLocaleString("en-US");
      }

      function clamp(value, min, max) {
        return Math.max(min, Math.min(max, value));
      }

      function estimatePayment(principal, apr, months) {
        if (principal <= 0 || months <= 0) return 0;
        const monthlyRate = apr / 100 / 12;
        if (monthlyRate <= 0) return principal / months;
        return principal * monthlyRate / (1 - Math.pow(1 + monthlyRate, -months));
      }

      function pressureColor(score) {
        if (score <= 24) return "#16a34a";
        if (score <= 49) return "#ca8a04";
        if (score <= 74) return "#ea580c";
        return "#dc2626";
      }

      function pressureLabel(score) {
        if (score <= 0) return "No negative-equity pressure";
        if (score <= 24) return "Low negative-equity pressure";
        if (score <= 49) return "Moderate negative-equity pressure";
        if (score <= 74) return "High negative-equity pressure";
        if (score < 100) return "Severe negative-equity pressure";
        return "Maximum negative-equity pressure";
      }

      button.addEventListener("click", function () {
        const currentPayoff = cleanNumber("currentPayoff");
        const tradeValue = cleanNumber("tradeValue");
        const cashToCover = cleanNumber("cashToCover");
        const newCarPrice = cleanNumber("newCarPrice");
        const taxesFees = cleanNumber("taxesFees");
        const downPayment = cleanNumber("downPayment");
        const apr = cleanNumber("apr");
        const termMonths = cleanNumber("termMonths");
        const income = cleanNumber("income");
        const essentials = cleanNumber("essentials");
        const otherDebt = cleanNumber("otherDebt");
        const emergencySavings = cleanNumber("emergencySavings");
        const insuranceChange = cleanNumber("insuranceChange");

        const currentCarNeed = document.getElementById("currentCarNeed").value;
        const loanStructure = document.getElementById("loanStructure").value;
        const incomeStability = document.getElementById("incomeStability").value;

        const results = document.getElementById("calcResults");
        const verdictEl = document.getElementById("calcVerdict");
        const bigMoneyEl = document.getElementById("bigMoneyResult");
        const pressureEl = document.getElementById("pressureResult");
        const loanMathEl = document.getElementById("loanMath");
        const paymentMathEl = document.getElementById("paymentMath");
        const savingsMathEl = document.getElementById("savingsMath");
        const analysisEl = document.getElementById("calcAnalysis");

        results.classList.add("active");

        if (
          currentPayoff <= 0 ||
          newCarPrice <= 0 ||
          income <= 0 ||
          termMonths <= 0 ||
          !currentCarNeed ||
          !loanStructure ||
          !incomeStability
        ) {
          verdictEl.innerText = "Add the missing details";
          bigMoneyEl.innerHTML =
            "<div style=\"" +
            moneyLabelStyle +
            "\">Negative equity rolled into new loan</div><div style=\"" +
            moneyValueStyle +
            "\">Need details</div><div style=\"" +
            moneySubtextStyle +
            "\">Enter current payoff, new vehicle price, income, loan term, and the dropdown details to calculate rolled negative equity.</div>";
          pressureEl.innerHTML = "";
          loanMathEl.innerText = "";
          paymentMathEl.innerText = "";
          savingsMathEl.innerText = "";
          analysisEl.innerText =
            "Trade value, cash to cover negative equity, fees, down payment, APR, essentials, debt, savings, and insurance change can be zero, but the calculator needs the core loan details.";
          return;
        }

        const rawNegativeEquity = Math.max(0, currentPayoff - tradeValue);
        const coveredNegativeEquity = Math.min(rawNegativeEquity, cashToCover);
        const rolledNegativeEquity = Math.max(0, rawNegativeEquity - coveredNegativeEquity);
        const cashLeftAfterCover = Math.max(0, emergencySavings - coveredNegativeEquity);
        const taxableDealBeforeDown = newCarPrice + taxesFees + rolledNegativeEquity;
        const newLoanAmount = Math.max(0, taxableDealBeforeDown - downPayment);
        const payment = estimatePayment(newLoanAmount, apr, termMonths);
        const loanToValue = newCarPrice > 0 ? newLoanAmount / newCarPrice : 0;
        const paymentShare = payment / income;
        const paymentPlusInsuranceShare = (payment + insuranceChange) / income;
        const obligations = essentials + otherDebt + payment + insuranceChange;
        const obligationShare = obligations / income;
        const monthlyRoom = income - obligations;
        const savingsMonthsAfter = essentials > 0 ? cashLeftAfterCover / Math.max(1, essentials) : 0;
        const negativeEquityShare = rolledNegativeEquity / Math.max(1, newCarPrice);
        const debtShare = otherDebt / income;

        let score = 0;

        if (rolledNegativeEquity <= 0) score = 5;
        else if (negativeEquityShare <= 0.05) score = 22;
        else if (negativeEquityShare <= 0.10) score = 38;
        else if (negativeEquityShare <= 0.20) score = 58;
        else if (negativeEquityShare <= 0.35) score = 76;
        else score = 92;

        if (loanToValue > 1.35) score += 18;
        else if (loanToValue > 1.20) score += 12;
        else if (loanToValue > 1.10) score += 7;
        else if (loanToValue <= 0.90) score -= 8;

        if (paymentPlusInsuranceShare > 0.24) score += 18;
        else if (paymentPlusInsuranceShare > 0.18) score += 10;
        else if (paymentPlusInsuranceShare > 0.12) score += 5;
        else if (paymentPlusInsuranceShare <= 0.08) score -= 6;

        if (termMonths >= 85) score += 16;
        else if (termMonths >= 73) score += 12;
        else if (termMonths >= 61) score += 6;
        else if (termMonths <= 48) score -= 4;

        if (apr >= 15) score += 18;
        else if (apr >= 10) score += 10;
        else if (apr >= 7) score += 5;
        else if (apr > 0 && apr <= 4) score -= 5;

        if (cashLeftAfterCover <= 0 && coveredNegativeEquity > 0) score += 24;
        else if (savingsMonthsAfter < 0.5) score += 18;
        else if (savingsMonthsAfter < 1) score += 12;
        else if (savingsMonthsAfter < 3) score += 6;
        else if (savingsMonthsAfter >= 24) score -= 22;
        else if (savingsMonthsAfter >= 6) score -= 14;
        else if (savingsMonthsAfter >= 3) score -= 7;

        if (obligationShare > 0.85) score += 18;
        else if (obligationShare > 0.70) score += 12;
        else if (obligationShare > 0.55) score += 6;
        else if (obligationShare < 0.35) score -= 6;

        if (monthlyRoom < 0) score += 25;
        else if (monthlyRoom < income * 0.08) score += 10;

        if (debtShare > 0.20) score += 10;
        else if (debtShare > 0.12) score += 5;

        if (loanStructure === "clean") score -= 7;
        if (loanStructure === "stretched") score += 10;
        if (loanStructure === "risky") score += 22;

        if (currentCarNeed === "works") score += 7;
        if (currentCarNeed === "urgent") score -= 4;

        if (incomeStability === "variable") score += 6;
        if (incomeStability === "unstable") score += 15;

        const noRolledDebtSafe =
          rolledNegativeEquity <= 0 &&
          savingsMonthsAfter >= 3 &&
          paymentPlusInsuranceShare <= 0.12 &&
          loanToValue <= 1.05 &&
          loanStructure !== "risky" &&
          incomeStability === "stable";

        const fullyCoveredWithStrongSavings =
          rolledNegativeEquity <= 0 &&
          rawNegativeEquity > 0 &&
          savingsMonthsAfter >= 6 &&
          paymentPlusInsuranceShare <= 0.14 &&
          loanToValue <= 1.10 &&
          loanStructure !== "risky" &&
          incomeStability === "stable";

        const hugeSavingsAndSafe =
          cashLeftAfterCover >= income * 12 &&
          savingsMonthsAfter >= 24 &&
          paymentPlusInsuranceShare <= 0.16 &&
          obligationShare <= 0.55 &&
          rolledNegativeEquity <= Math.max(newCarPrice * 0.10, income * 2) &&
          loanStructure !== "risky" &&
          incomeStability === "stable";

        const cashFortressAndSafe =
          emergencySavings >= income * 12 &&
          emergencySavings >= newLoanAmount * 2 &&
          cashLeftAfterCover >= income * 6 &&
          paymentPlusInsuranceShare <= 0.16 &&
          loanStructure !== "risky" &&
          incomeStability === "stable";

        if (noRolledDebtSafe || fullyCoveredWithStrongSavings || hugeSavingsAndSafe || cashFortressAndSafe) {
          score = 0;
        }

        const richIncomeAndSafe =
          income >= Math.max(newLoanAmount, rolledNegativeEquity, newCarPrice, 100000) * 50 &&
          paymentPlusInsuranceShare <= 0.03 &&
          obligationShare <= 0.10 &&
          debtShare <= 0.05 &&
          loanStructure !== "risky" &&
          incomeStability === "stable";

        if (richIncomeAndSafe) {
          score = 0;
        }



        if (
          income <= 0 ||
          (rolledNegativeEquity >= newCarPrice * 0.30 && termMonths >= 72 && savingsMonthsAfter < 1) ||
          (monthlyRoom < 0 && rolledNegativeEquity > 0 && savingsMonthsAfter < 1) ||
          (loanStructure === "risky" && loanToValue >= 1.35 && savingsMonthsAfter < 1) ||
          (paymentPlusInsuranceShare >= 0.35 && savingsMonthsAfter < 1)
        ) {
          score = 100;
        }

        score = Math.round(clamp(score, 0, 100));

        const color = pressureColor(score);
        const pressureName = pressureLabel(score);
        let verdict = pressureName;
        let analysis = "";

        if (score === 0) {
          analysis =
            "The negative-equity problem is either gone or safely covered, savings remain objectively strong, and the new payment does not create meaningful pressure. This is the kind of case where pressure should fall to zero.";
        } else if (rolledNegativeEquity <= 0) {
          analysis =
            "No negative equity is being rolled into the new loan based on the inputs. The next step is still to pressure-test the normal car payment, insurance, and total ownership cost.";
        } else if (score <= 24) {
          analysis =
            "The rolled balance appears manageable, but it is still old debt attached to a new vehicle. Keep the loan term and add-ons under control.";
        } else if (score <= 49) {
          analysis =
            "The deal has moderate underwater risk. The payment may work, but loan-to-value, term length, and cash cushion should be checked before signing.";
        } else if (score <= 74) {
          analysis =
            "The trade is creating high pressure. Consider paying down the current loan, selling privately, choosing a cheaper replacement, or waiting longer.";
        } else if (score < 100) {
          analysis =
            "The negative equity is severe enough that the new loan may begin in a weak position. This can keep the next car underwater and make the following trade harder.";
        } else {
          analysis =
            "The inputs show maximum pressure. Large rolled debt, thin savings, high monthly burden, or risky loan structure make this a deal to avoid or rebuild from scratch.";
        }

        verdictEl.innerText = verdict;

        bigMoneyEl.innerHTML =
          "<div style=\"" +
          moneyLabelStyle +
          "\">Negative equity rolled into new loan</div><div style=\"" +
          moneyValueStyle +
          "\">" +
          money(rolledNegativeEquity) +
          "</div><div style=\"" +
          moneySubtextStyle +
          "\">Raw negative equity is " +
          money(rawNegativeEquity) +
          ". Cash, rebate, reimbursement, or outside help covers " +
          money(coveredNegativeEquity) +
          " before the new loan is built.</div>";

        pressureEl.innerHTML =
          "<div style=\"margin: 18px 0 18px;\"><div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 12px; align-items: center; margin-bottom: 8px;\"><strong style=\"color: var(--text);\">Negative-equity pressure score</strong><strong style=\"color:" +
          color +
          ";\">" +
          score +
          "/100</strong></div><div style=\"height: 14px; border-radius: 999px; background: #e5e7eb; overflow: hidden;\"><div style=\"height: 100%; width:" +
          score +
          "%; background:" +
          color +
          ";\"></div></div></div>";

        loanMathEl.innerText =
          "Loan math: new loan amount is about " +
          money(newLoanAmount) +
          " after vehicle price " +
          money(newCarPrice) +
          ", taxes and fees " +
          money(taxesFees) +
          ", rolled negative equity " +
          money(rolledNegativeEquity) +
          ", and down payment " +
          money(downPayment) +
          ". Loan-to-value is about " +
          (loanToValue * 100).toFixed(1) +
          "% of the new vehicle price.";

        paymentMathEl.innerText =
          "Payment test: estimated new payment is about " +
          money(payment) +
          " per month at " +
          apr.toFixed(2) +
          "% APR for " +
          Math.round(termMonths) +
          " months. Payment plus insurance increase equals about " +
          (paymentPlusInsuranceShare * 100).toFixed(1) +
          "% of take-home income.";

        savingsMathEl.innerText =
          "Savings test: cash left after covering negative equity is about " +
          money(cashLeftAfterCover) +
          ", covering about " +
          savingsMonthsAfter.toFixed(1) +
          " months of essential expenses. Monthly room after essentials, other debt, new payment, and insurance change is about " +
          money(monthlyRoom) +
          ".";

        analysisEl.innerText = analysis;
      });
    });
  </script>
  <SourcesAssumptionsBlock category="Cars" />
</Layout>